BODY = '''<h2>What the CDC and MedlinePlus Say</h2>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that about 37% of people who fall report an injury that needed medical treatment or limited their activity for at least a day.</p>
<p>The CDC's "Check for Safety" checklist has two lines for the bedroom that apply to night-time trips. If the light near the bed is hard to reach, it suggests a lamp close to the bed. If the path from the bed to the bathroom is dark, it suggests a nightlight so you can see where you are walking. A commode shortens that path, and the same advice about light still applies to it.</p>
<p>MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page, says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls, and names a raised seat that fits over the toilet or a commode chair as two ways to do it. Neither source tells you which commode to buy, so use the measurements below, and ask a doctor or occupational therapist if you are unsure about the transfer.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Put it where you can reach it without stepping over anything.</strong> Keep the path from the bed clear, and put a lamp or nightlight where you can reach it from the bed, as the CDC suggests.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Bedside Commode</h2>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>Four of the five are rated for 350 pounds. The DMI is rated for 500 pounds on its listing. Choose a limit above the weight of the person using it.</p>
<h3>Seat height</h3>
<p>Only the Drive RTL11158KDR gives a height range on the maker's page, 17 to 23 inches. The Medline drop arm lists 18 to 22 inches and the DMI 20 to 24 inches on their listings. Set the height so the seat is level with the bed edge or a little above it, and check that the legs are locked at the same setting on all four sides.</p>
<h3>Fixed arms or drop arms</h3>
<p>Fixed arms give you something to push on and cannot move out of the way. Drop arms swing down so you can slide across from a wheelchair or the bed. The Medline drop arm and the DMI have them.</p>
<h3>Seat size</h3>
<p>The seats run from 13.5 inches wide on the Drive 11148-1 to 23.25 inches on the DMI. Compare the width with the person's hips and the space beside the bed.</p>
<h3>Bucket and cleaning</h3>
<p>The Drive models come with a 7.5-quart bucket with a cover and splash shield, and the Drive RTL11158KDR adds a carry handle. The DMI bucket is 5 liters with a lid and handle. Empty it after each use.</p>
<h3>Use as a raised seat</h3>
<p>The Medline and Drive 3-in-1 listings say the commode can go over a toilet as a raised seat or safety frame, with the bucket removed. Only the listings say so, so read the maker's instructions for that use before you rely on it.</p>

<h2>Bedside Commode Questions</h2>
<h3>Who needs a bedside commode?</h3>
<p>People who find the walk to the bathroom too far or too dark, or who are recovering from surgery or an illness. MedlinePlus lists a commode chair as one way to raise the toilet seat height, which it says can help prevent falls. A doctor or occupational therapist can say whether you need one.</p>
<h3>What is the difference between fixed and drop arms?</h3>
<p>Fixed arms stay put. Drop arms lower, so you can slide across from a wheelchair or the side of a bed. The Medline drop arm and the DMI are the two drop-arm models here.</p>
<h3>Can a commode go over a toilet?</h3>
<p>Some can. The Medline 3-in-1 and the Drive RTL11158KDR are described in their listings as usable as a raised toilet seat or a safety frame, and the Medline drop arm listing says to remove the bucket for that use. Drive's own page does not mention it, so check the maker's instructions.</p>
<h3>How do I make the walk safer at night?</h3>
<p>The CDC suggests a lamp close to the bed and a nightlight on the path to the bathroom. Keep the floor clear, and see our guides to <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seats</a> and <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a> for the bathroom itself.</p>
<h3>How do I clean and empty the bucket?</h3>
<p>The Drive models come with a 7.5-quart bucket with a cover, a splash shield and a carry handle on the RTL11158KDR, and the Drive listing suggests liners for faster clean-up. The DMI listing says its 5-liter bucket lifts out for emptying and can be rinsed with mild soap and water. Empty it after each use, and follow the maker's cleaning instructions for the frame and seat.</p>
<h3>How high should I set the seat?</h3>
<p>Drive's page gives a seat height of 17 to 23 inches for the RTL11158KDR, and the listings give 18 to 22 inches for the Medline drop arm and 20 to 24 for the DMI. A common starting point is a seat level with the edge of the bed or a little above it, so you can slide across and stand without a drop. Set all four legs to the same hole and check that the push pins are locked.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Drive Medical Folding Bedside Commode is the best pick for most people. It is rated for 350 pounds, Drive's page gives its size and weight, and 37,079 reviews average 4.6 stars. The trade is a fixed pair of arms and a seat height that Drive does not state, so check the settings when you build it.</p>
<p>The Drive RTL11158KDR is worth the small extra cost if you want the height range in writing, 17 to 23 inches, and a carry handle on the bucket. The Medline 3-in-1 adds an elongated seat and 19 inches between the arms, but it has only 179 reviews and no maker page we could read.</p>
<p>If you move sideways, spend more. The Medline drop arm has padded arms that lower and a foam seat, and the DMI has a 23.25-inch seat and a 500-pound rating. Measure the space beside the bed before you buy the DMI, since it is 29.5 inches wide.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Drive Medical Folding Bedside Commode is the best all-round bedside commode, with the Drive RTL11158KDR for a stated height range. Choose the Medline drop arm for transfers from a wheelchair, and the DMI for an extra-wide seat and 500 pounds. Put a lamp and a nightlight on the path as the CDC suggests, and see our guides to <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seats</a> and <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a>.</p>'''

